# Frontend Code Quality

This is an existing Angular application. Prefer simple, readable code that follows existing Angular patterns.

## Prefer

- Simple code over clever code
- Readable code over highly abstract code
- Small cohesive components and minimal abstractions
- Strong TypeScript typing
- Deriving state rather than duplicating it
- Declarative Angular templates and straightforward RxJS
- Reuse when it genuinely reduces duplication

## Avoid

- Unnecessary wrapper components, services, abstractions, helper functions, or generic utilities
- Excessive defensive programming
- Duplicate state, unnecessary RxJS subjects, and unnecessary subscriptions
- `any`
- Premature optimization
- Patterns added only because they are fashionable
- New code when existing code can be reused
- Rewriting working functionality without a concrete benefit

## Changing existing code

1. Understand the existing implementation first.
2. Make the smallest change that solves the problem.
3. Preserve existing behavior.
4. Prefer deletion over adding another abstraction.
5. Do not increase complexity without a clear reason.

Before creating a component, service, utility, interface, abstraction, or state container, check whether existing code can be reused. Unnecessary code is technical debt.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — new subject and subscription for state the template can derive
readonly items$ = new BehaviorSubject<Item[]>([]);
ngOnInit(): void {
  this.service.load().subscribe(items => this.items$.next(items));
}

// ✅ GOOD — expose the existing stream and derive in the template
readonly items$ = this.service.load();
```
